course: dictionary
lesson: 12903
title: Eroded: worn away slowly
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: When wind, water, ice or waves wear something away bit by bit, we say it is eroded. «The sea eroded the cliff over many years.» The sea did not knock the cliff down in one go. It wore it away slowly, a little at a time.
explain: Rivers, rain and wind can erode rocks and soil too. «The river eroded a deep canyon through the rock.» Erode is the verb, and eroded is its past tense: erode, erodes, eroded, eroding.
explain: Words can be eroded too. «Her confidence was eroded by all the unkind comments.» Nobody chipped pieces off her confidence, but it became weaker little by little. Ask yourself: is nature wearing something away, or is something being made weaker bit by bit?
fact: Erode means to wear away slowly.
fact: Wind, water, ice and waves can all erode rock, soil, cliffs and beaches.
fact: Erosion is the noun for this slow wearing away.
fact: «Eroded» can also mean made weaker little by little, like trust or confidence.
fact: Erosion carries bits of rock away, and deposition is when those bits are dropped somewhere new.
exercise: «The sea eroded the cliff over many years.» What does eroded mean here? | Painted bright white | Frozen solid in one night | *Worn away slowly by the waves | Built up into a tall tower
exercise: Which of these is a natural force that can erode rock? | A chair | A paintbrush | A pencil | *Rain
exercise: «Her confidence was eroded by the teasing.» What does eroded suggest? | *Made weaker, little by little | Added to, like a pile of coins | Made stronger in one big jump | Painted over with bright colours
test: «The river eroded a deep canyon through the rock.» Which word shows the action of wearing away? | river | *eroded | canyon | rock
test: Which noun names the slow wearing away of rock and soil? | Eruption | *Erosion | Earthquake | Edge
exam: Which sentence uses eroded in the figurative sense? | *«Doubt eroded her confidence, bit by bit.» | «The waves eroded the sandy beach.» | «The ice eroded the valley over centuries.» | «The rocks eroded into smooth pebbles.»
